WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.878 --> 00:00:14.544
[Martin Luther King Jr.] The fact is

00:00:14.544 --> 00:00:15.377
that millions of Negroes,

00:00:15.377 --> 00:00:19.970
as a result of centuries
of denial and neglect,

00:00:21.005 --> 00:00:22.493
have been left bootless.

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:39.290
It is a cruel jest to
say to a bootless man

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:42.063
that he ought to lift himself
by his own bootstraps.

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:53.650
At the dawn of emancipation,

00:00:53.650 --> 00:00:58.650
the total Black wealth in
America was about 0.5%.

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:04.513
Today that number is between 1-2%.

00:01:04.513 --> 00:01:07.793
So there has been practically no movement.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:11.840
The most pernicious form

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:15.260
of the racial wealth gap
is when we take people,

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:18.040
human beings, as capital for others,

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:21.940
as being an asset by which
others can be enriched,

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:25.580
can accumulate, can pass down resources

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:27.443
from one generation to the other.

00:01:30.312 --> 00:01:32.430
[Martin Luther King Jr.]
They find themselves

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:37.358
impoverished aliens in
this affluent society

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:43.030
and there is a great deal
that society can and must do

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:49.000
if the Negro is to gain the
economic security that he needs.

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:02.030
The origins of the
racial wealth gap begins

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:05.810
when Black people entered the
shores of the United States

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:07.853
as chattel capital.

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:10.500
There were two forms

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:12.960
in which Black people served as capital,

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:17.320
the uncompensated labor
that they provided,

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:20.850
but their sheer bodies
themselves could be traded,

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:24.720
could be traded and stood
as a source of wealth

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:27.753
and wealth building for White people.

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:34.880
The enslavement of Black people in America

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:38.140
is what made the U.S. economy grow

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:40.363
into the powerhouse that it is today.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:44.950
Even though most of this
farming activity was going on

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:47.730
in the southern part of the United States,

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:51.550
businesses all over the
country were involved in this.

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:55.470
Insurance companies insured
the slaves themselves.

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:57.620
There were even slave-backed securities

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:00.463
that could be traded by
financial institutions.

00:03:02.690 --> 00:03:06.380
In essence, slavery
encompassed nearly every part

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:08.323
of the U.S. economy at this point.

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:13.350
At the start of the Civil War,

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:18.030
each slave was worth an average of $1,000.

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:20.910
That came to $4 billion at that point

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:24.930
because there were around four
million slaves in the U.S.

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:29.750
In today's money, that
comes to $42 trillion

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:33.460
and $42 trillion is about double the size

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:35.573
of the current U.S. economy.

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:47.680
With the 13th Amendment that freed slaves,

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:51.070
the point is that, that's not enough.

00:03:51.070 --> 00:03:55.560
When you've had centuries
of literally bondage imposed

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:59.690
upon a people, if you want
true justice or true freedom,

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:03.650
there needed to be not only
that freedom from bondage,

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:06.890
but actual accompanying of resources

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:10.250
so that people could
pursue their livelihood.

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:13.490
Expecting a people to
to thrive and flourish

00:04:13.490 --> 00:04:16.470
without compensation
for all that unjust harm

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:20.940
really just set up a system for oppression

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:23.053
and a system that was unjust.

00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:27.610
Toward the end of the Civil War,

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:31.770
General William Tecumseh
Sherman made a promise to slaves

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:33.830
that as soon as the war was over,

00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:37.563
they would be granted 40
acres of land and a mule.

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:42.070
This idea of giving
land had a dual purpose.

00:04:42.070 --> 00:04:44.100
People that supported it saw it as a way

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:48.680
to both free the formerly-enslaved
from their old masters

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:50.760
and as a way to dilute the power

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:52.913
of these southern land owners.

00:04:54.040 --> 00:04:55.210
But a few months later,

00:04:55.210 --> 00:04:58.580
President Abraham Lincoln
was shot and killed.

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:00.320
His Vice President, Johnson,

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:03.030
swiftly got rid of this promise.

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:05.510
When Andrew Johnson vetoed
the Freedmen's Bill,

00:05:05.510 --> 00:05:07.300
he said, look, this is a
White man's government,

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:09.160
so that was the impetus.

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:10.500
But also used this idea

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:12.690
that the government
doesn't give away land,

00:05:12.690 --> 00:05:13.770
this is capitalism.

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:16.740
And the idea that this is a
free market and laissez faire

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:21.280
was very much used to undercut
claims of the 40 acres,

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:22.810
while at the same time

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:26.150
the government is giving
away free land out west

00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:27.450
through the Homestead Act.

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:30.900
The Homestead Acts were a series of bills

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:34.220
meant to encourage settlement
of the American West.

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:38.600
In all, 270 million acres were given out

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:40.570
as part of the bills.

00:05:40.570 --> 00:05:42.850
That land, which accounts for about 10%

00:05:42.850 --> 00:05:44.730
of the entire country,

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:47.110
was mostly stolen from Native Americans

00:05:47.110 --> 00:05:50.230
and most of the 1.5 million Americans

00:05:50.230 --> 00:05:53.610
that benefited from the
Homestead Acts were White.

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:55.250
They were literally giveaways

00:05:55.250 --> 00:05:59.540
that provided White people
ultimately a key ingredient

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:02.080
to build wealth, and
that's capital itself,

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:04.683
and by design Black
people were not included.

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:09.610
The Homestead Act is a dramatic indicator

00:06:09.610 --> 00:06:14.290
of U.S. government
facilitating the accumulation

00:06:14.290 --> 00:06:18.140
for White people and not
facilitating it for Black people,

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:19.580
but it's one of many.

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:22.430
The Homestead Act fits
into a larger context

00:06:22.430 --> 00:06:24.990
that throughout our United States history,

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:28.950
well beyond slavery, we
think about racial repression

00:06:28.950 --> 00:06:31.550
and we think that with emancipation,

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:33.910
that all of a sudden
we were a free country,

00:06:33.910 --> 00:06:37.420
but a bigger point to make is that

00:06:37.420 --> 00:06:39.050
if we think about a middle class,

00:06:39.050 --> 00:06:43.120
a middle, White, asset-building class,

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:45.453
it was government policy
that generated it.

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:51.040
What little economic successes

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:53.730
Black people were able
to have at this time

00:06:53.730 --> 00:06:56.933
often spurred a lot of
resentment among Whites.

00:07:02.540 --> 00:07:06.300
This led to thousands of
lynchings of Black people

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:08.430
and other violent attacks,

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:12.280
sometimes on entire Black
neighborhoods and communities.

00:07:12.280 --> 00:07:13.927
My name is Roy Woodle

00:07:13.927 --> 00:07:15.923
and I'm not ashamed to be a Klansmen.

00:07:15.923 --> 00:07:16.756
Amen!

00:07:21.730 --> 00:07:23.860
This was in the time of heavy Jim Crow,

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:26.480
it was in the time of rising Klan,

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:28.930
and in the time where
White supremacy was gaining

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:31.960
this pseudo-scientific legitimacy.

00:07:34.090 --> 00:07:36.810
It is the case that throughout history,

00:07:36.810 --> 00:07:39.250
not only have Blacks not been permitted

00:07:39.250 --> 00:07:43.980
to accumulate land, capital
and other forms of resource

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:45.940
to the same extent as Whites,

00:07:45.940 --> 00:07:48.550
but when they were able
to overcome barriers

00:07:48.550 --> 00:07:52.470
and accumulate those resources,
it's always been subject

00:07:52.470 --> 00:07:55.690
to outright terror, outright seizure,

00:07:55.690 --> 00:07:58.350
because we did not have the codification

00:07:58.350 --> 00:08:00.310
that comes along with government

00:08:00.310 --> 00:08:02.950
to protect the resources of Black people

00:08:02.950 --> 00:08:05.700
in the same way that it was
protected for White people.

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:11.970
Tulsa, for example, in the
1900s, there was oil boom

00:08:11.970 --> 00:08:14.330
with a lot of wealth that
was in that community

00:08:14.330 --> 00:08:16.160
and so they built the Greenwood District

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:19.170
and the central part of the
city was this Black church.

00:08:19.170 --> 00:08:21.280
It was very ornate and beautiful.

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:23.070
These places are called
the Black Wall Street,

00:08:23.070 --> 00:08:24.940
where it wasn't a sharecropping market,

00:08:24.940 --> 00:08:26.340
there were landholdings.

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:31.203
The thing that happened was
that the racial resentment grew.

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:36.340
There were bombings

00:08:36.340 --> 00:08:41.150
and really just a complete
destruction of that city

00:08:41.150 --> 00:08:43.913
and very much stemming
from that resentment.

00:08:46.340 --> 00:08:47.500
In that one day,

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:49.270
Black Wall Street was destroyed

00:08:49.270 --> 00:08:52.700
and 10,000 Tulsans were displaced.

00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:55.660
There's even evidence that
World War One bomber planes

00:08:55.660 --> 00:08:57.723
were used to burn the town.

00:09:06.030 --> 00:09:07.010
In Tulsa alone,

00:09:07.010 --> 00:09:10.710
the property damage amounts
to about $200 million

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:11.853
in today's money.

00:09:12.930 --> 00:09:15.970
The Tulsa Massacre was
not an isolated incident.

00:09:15.970 --> 00:09:17.630
About 100 of these occurred

00:09:17.630 --> 00:09:21.150
between the end of the
Civil War and the 1940s,

00:09:21.150 --> 00:09:24.093
destroying a substantial
amount of Black wealth.

00:09:25.700 --> 00:09:26.770
On the one hand,

00:09:26.770 --> 00:09:29.480
those communities are clear examples

00:09:29.480 --> 00:09:33.610
of the ways in which Black
people have demonstrated

00:09:33.610 --> 00:09:37.163
they can not only survive,
but they can actually thrive.

00:09:39.290 --> 00:09:43.090
But on the other hand,
we romanticize the past.

00:09:43.090 --> 00:09:48.090
We describe a nostalgia as if
we had these kingdoms of gold,

00:09:48.108 --> 00:09:50.680
when in reality, it was never the case

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:53.223
that Black people had wealth en masse.

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:57.000
That said, left to our own druthers,

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:59.533
the wealth gap is not gonna be solved.

00:10:03.350 --> 00:10:07.110
Slavery itself created a
multi-trillion dollar wealth gap

00:10:07.110 --> 00:10:09.620
and the period immediately
following slavery

00:10:09.620 --> 00:10:11.385
didn't do anything to close it.

00:10:13.470 --> 00:10:15.360
And none of that includes the one thing

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:17.430
that most people today

00:10:17.430 --> 00:10:21.553
can trace their wealth
back to: Home ownership.

00:10:24.690 --> 00:10:26.670
Following the Great Depression,

00:10:26.670 --> 00:10:30.560
Congress passed a series of
laws to help the country recover

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:32.490
from the economic crisis.

00:10:32.490 --> 00:10:36.620
Among them was the 1934
National Housing Act.

00:10:36.620 --> 00:10:39.650
This law, for the first time,
made home ownership affordable

00:10:39.650 --> 00:10:41.780
to working class individuals.

00:10:41.780 --> 00:10:44.100
And that was a good
thing, except for the fact

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:46.690
that it intentionally
excluded Black people.

00:10:46.690 --> 00:10:49.520
Then mortgage companies did
not lend to Black people.

00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:52.330
In fact, the federal
government went the other way.

00:10:52.330 --> 00:10:55.430
The federal government, as
well as local government,

00:10:55.430 --> 00:10:57.580
they facilitated the process

00:10:57.580 --> 00:11:00.910
of excluding Black people
from these products

00:11:00.910 --> 00:11:02.270
and how did they do so?

00:11:02.270 --> 00:11:04.356
Neighborhoods were redlined.

00:11:05.270 --> 00:11:07.690
The government created
maps that showed the level

00:11:07.690 --> 00:11:09.560
of risk by neighborhood.

00:11:09.560 --> 00:11:11.300
These had different colors

00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:14.550
and the riskiest was outlined in red.

00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:16.900
This was very much a crude calculus

00:11:16.900 --> 00:11:18.350
but sophisticated in a way

00:11:18.350 --> 00:11:21.830
that they actually had tons
of maps across the country

00:11:21.830 --> 00:11:23.830
and you would see the way

00:11:23.830 --> 00:11:25.870
that they mapped out a neighborhood

00:11:25.870 --> 00:11:28.331
and they looked at the
house: Is it single-family?

00:11:28.331 --> 00:11:29.240
Is it industrial?

00:11:29.240 --> 00:11:30.670
Is it farmland?

00:11:30.670 --> 00:11:32.710
What are the characteristics
of the neighborhood?

00:11:32.710 --> 00:11:34.530
But the number one thing
that they looked at

00:11:34.530 --> 00:11:36.000
is who lives there?

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:38.700
So they would say this is a
Black neighborhood, redline.

00:11:38.700 --> 00:11:40.810
And naturally what would develop?

00:11:40.810 --> 00:11:41.643
Slums.

00:11:41.643 --> 00:11:44.220
What would develop?
Neighborhood degregation.

00:11:44.220 --> 00:11:46.770
When you have capital
fleeing certain areas

00:11:46.770 --> 00:11:49.290
and capital being flooded into areas,

00:11:49.290 --> 00:11:51.623
the outcomes become obvious.

00:11:52.630 --> 00:11:54.900
If you lived in a Black neighborhood,

00:11:54.900 --> 00:11:56.730
because it was a Black neighborhood,

00:11:56.730 --> 00:11:58.450
you did not get that FHA mortgage,

00:11:58.450 --> 00:12:00.090
you did not get that GI bill loan,

00:12:00.090 --> 00:12:01.790
you did not get the farm loans.

00:12:01.790 --> 00:12:04.100
So today the legacy of those laws

00:12:04.100 --> 00:12:06.880
is that your neighborhood
is very much racially-coded.

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:09.860
This is how racism gets
embedded in wealth.

00:12:09.860 --> 00:12:11.390
Word came from Capitol Hill

00:12:11.390 --> 00:12:14.450
that Congress had passed
and sent to the White House

00:12:14.450 --> 00:12:16.853
the Civil Rights Act of 1968.

00:12:18.610 --> 00:12:22.330
Included in the measure was
a landmark open housing bill,

00:12:22.330 --> 00:12:25.700
which, when fully effective,
would forbid discrimination

00:12:25.700 --> 00:12:28.940
in approximately 80%
of all housing offered

00:12:28.940 --> 00:12:32.023
for rent or for sale in the United States.

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:34.380
The Civil Rights Movement,

00:12:34.380 --> 00:12:37.920
and in particular the
Fair Housing Act of 1968,

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:40.650
tried to put an end to
that discrimination.

00:12:40.650 --> 00:12:44.070
Fair housing for all,

00:12:44.070 --> 00:12:48.931
all human beings who live in this country,

00:12:50.660 --> 00:12:54.395
is now a part of the American way of life.

00:12:55.992 --> 00:12:58.680
But they didn't have much economic power

00:12:58.680 --> 00:13:01.350
and they struggled to
undo the decades of damage

00:13:01.350 --> 00:13:02.773
that had already been done.

00:13:05.380 --> 00:13:08.870
Since that time the wealth
gap has actually widened.

00:13:08.870 --> 00:13:13.150
In 1967, Black families had 1/5 the wealth

00:13:13.150 --> 00:13:14.640
of White families.

00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:17.200
Today, that number is 1/7.

00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:19.560
And that really is the legacy
of the racial wealth gap.

00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:23.280
It's not just that you have
more money in the bank account,

00:13:23.280 --> 00:13:26.080
your house is higher value.

00:13:26.080 --> 00:13:28.470
It is also the opportunities;

00:13:28.470 --> 00:13:30.750
the ladders up for your kids are different

00:13:30.750 --> 00:13:31.820
in certain neighborhoods,

00:13:31.820 --> 00:13:33.690
non-existent in some neighborhoods

00:13:33.690 --> 00:13:37.660
and, quite frankly, hard to fall off of

00:13:37.660 --> 00:13:38.810
in other neighborhoods.

00:13:40.020 --> 00:13:42.330
This story is not all dismal.

00:13:42.330 --> 00:13:44.850
Just like policy can be enacted

00:13:44.850 --> 00:13:47.850
to create these unjust disparities,

00:13:47.850 --> 00:13:51.063
likewise policy can be
enacted to rectify them.

00:13:52.300 --> 00:13:54.970
What we need today is a new policy regime

00:13:54.970 --> 00:13:58.780
that provides that resource
in a way that's anti-racist

00:13:58.780 --> 00:14:01.460
so that we all can have the opportunity

00:14:01.460 --> 00:14:05.780
for capital accumulation and
intergenerational transfers.

00:14:05.780 --> 00:14:09.210
We need something in
perpetuity that ensures

00:14:09.210 --> 00:14:11.890
that as a birthright

00:14:11.890 --> 00:14:16.890
no American is excluded
from the economic security

00:14:17.420 --> 00:14:19.863
that comes along with a capital endowment.

